  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "wx_c",
      "defType": "meta/comment",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": -344
      },
      "params": {
        "text": "Weather map\n\nA pressure field — two octaves of slowly drifting noise plus a storm's low — drawn the way a forecaster would. Contours traces it twice: filled bands and the isobars every 3 hPa. The wind is geostrophic: it blows along the isobars (round the low, out of the highs), faster where they crowd, computed from the field's slope in an Expression. That wind feeds three layers: Contours' flow lines, 320 drifting particles (Read* / Write* state, Echo trails) and a grid of arrows. H and L mark the highest and lowest pressure on the map.\n\n• XY pad: where the storm sits.  • Slider into `s`: the storm's depth (hPa).\n• Slider into `k`: wind speed.  • Vary: a new weather pattern.",
        "width": 560,
        "height": 280
      },
      "portValues": {
        "text": "Weather map\n\nA pressure field — two octaves of slowly drifting noise plus a storm's low — drawn the way a forecaster would. Contours traces it twice: filled bands and the isobars every 3 hPa. The wind is geostrophic: it blows along the isobars (round the low, out of the highs), faster where they crowd, computed from the field's slope in an Expression. That wind feeds three layers: Contours' flow lines, 320 drifting particles (Read* / Write* state, Echo trails) and a grid of arrows. H and L mark the highest and lowest pressure on the map.\n\n• XY pad: where the storm sits.  • Slider into `s`: the storm's depth (hPa).\n• Slider into `k`: wind speed.  • Vary: a new weather pattern.",
        "width": 560,
        "height": 280
      }
    },
